<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Cantiaci</h1><p class="st-meta">55 BCE – 43 CE (approx.) · capital Durovernum Cantiacorum (Canterbury)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The people of Kent, first named by Julius Caesar, whose four kings attacked his ships in 54 BCE and whose coast saw the Roman landing of 43.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kent was the part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> described its people as the most civilised of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a>, much like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> in custom. When he crossed in 55 and 54 BCE, Cantium was divided among four kings, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cingetorix-cantium">Cingetorix</a>, Carvilius, Taximagulus and Segovax, who attacked his beached fleet at the command of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassivellaunus">Cassivellaunus</a> and were driven off.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kent was densely farmed and closely tied to the Continent, receiving Gallo-Belgic coins and producing its own issues, including early cast potin coins. Oppida grew at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rochester-kent">Rochester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bigbury-camp">Bigbury</a>. In the decades before the Claudian invasion, coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubnovellaunus">Dubnovellaunus</a> and later the influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a> suggest Kent fell under the sway of more powerful northern neighbours.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Roman army landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richborough">Richborough</a> in 43, and Kent was quickly absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>. Its people formed the civitas of the Cantiaci, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Durovernum Cantiacorum</a>, the later Canterbury. The old name lived on in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kent">Kingdom of Kent</a> and the modern county.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cantiaci.jpg" alt="Cantiaci" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Jpb1301 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Cantiaci.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>55 BCE – 43 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Durovernum Cantiacorum (Canterbury)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cingetorix, Carvilius, Taximagulus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cantii, Kingdom of Cantium</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-6&amp;focus=cantiaci" aria-label="Cantiaci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4705 401 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cantiaci lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5025 657L5304 942M5025 657L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5025 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5025 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Durovernum Cantiacorum. <a href="/atlas?year=-6&amp;focus=cantiaci">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
